 "Welcome to the final boss of World 3!\n\nWe prove the fact that sits at the heart of the **Euclidean Algorithm**: shifting a number by a multiple of §0 does not change its greatest common divisor with §1.\n\nFormulation: if §2 is a (non-negative) gcd of §3 and §4, and §5 is a (non-negative) gcd of §6 and §7, then §8.\n\n**Syntax Tip:** Before we start, a quick trick! Since §9 is a nested logical AND (§10), you can extract its pieces directly using **dot notation** without destroying the original hypothesis. For example, §11 gets the very first fact, and §12 gets the second. Because we will need to pass the intact hypotheses §13 and §14 to §15 later, using dot notation is safer here than using §16!\n\n**Strategy:** This one's long — take it one step at a time!\n\n1. Show §17 is also a common divisor of §18 and §19 (not just of §20): since §21, and §22 divides both §23 and §24, it divides §25 too. Now §26 is a common divisor of §27 and §28, and §29 is *the greatest one* (Level 3!), so §30.\n2. Symmetrically, show §31 is also a common divisor of §32 and §33: since §34 divides both §35 and §36, it divides the combination §37 too. Applying Level 3 again (this time with §38 as \"the greatest\"), you get §39.\n3. Finally, §40 and §41, together with §42 and §43, give §44 by the antisymmetry lemma (§45) that has just been added to your inventory!":
